/**
\defgroup memory Memory
\brief `TObjectPtr<T>`, the reference-counted pointer most engine APIs use to pass objects around.

<table class="markdownTable">
<tr><td><b>Source</b></td><td>\src{include/Memory/Janitors.hpp} · \srcdir{src/Memory}</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Include</b></td><td>`#include <Memory/Module.hpp>` (or `<Memory/Janitors.hpp>`)</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Namespace</b></td><td>`TObjectPtr` and `CastTo` are global</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Started by</b></td><td>Nothing to start: `Memory::Module()` is empty and not called by the engine</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Example</b></td><td>\ref ex_assets</td></tr>
</table>

## How to use

`TObjectPtr<T>` owns a heap-allocated `T` together with a use count. Copies share the object; when the last copy goes away, the object is deleted. In Debug builds each allocation is reported to the Tracy profiler's memory view.

\genexopen{modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp,TObjectPtr in practice}
\snippet modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp memory_types
\snippet modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp object_ptr
\endex

| You write | You get |
| --- | --- |
| `TObjectPtr<T> p;` | **A new `T`**, default-constructed. A default `TObjectPtr` is never empty. |
| `TObjectPtr<T> p = nullptr;` | An empty pointer. `p.IsValid()` is `false`. |
| `TObjectPtr<T> p(args...);` | A new `T` built from `args` (the constructor is `explicit`). |
| `TObjectPtr<T> p = someT;` | A new `T` copied from `someT`. |
| `TObjectPtr<T> q = p;` | A second reference to the same object. |
| `TObjectPtr<Base> b = derivedPtr;` | A reference to the same object, as its base type. |
| `CastTo<Derived>(basePtr)` | A reference to the same object, as a derived type. Unchecked, like `static_cast`. |
| `p.Clear();` or `p = nullptr;` | Drops this reference; deletes the object if it was the last. |
| `p->Member`, `p.Raw()` | Access the object. `Raw()` returns the plain pointer. |
| `p == q` | `true` if both point to the same object. |

The engine uses `TObjectPtr` for assets (`TObjectPtr<Graphics::STexture2D>`), registry entries (`TObjectPtr<Filesystem::SAssetData>`), editor tools and render layers.

## Things to watch out for

- **`TObjectPtr<T> x;` allocates.** Write `= nullptr` when you want an empty pointer, for example for a member that is filled in later.
- **Assigning over a pointer that already holds an object does not release that object** (\src{include/Memory/Janitors.hpp,95,111}). The old object is never deleted. Call `Clear()` first if the pointer may already hold something you want released.
- Constructing from a `T&&` copies the object instead of moving it (\src{include/Memory/Janitors.hpp,40}).
- `CastTo` on an empty pointer crashes: it increments the use count without checking (\src{include/Memory/Janitors.hpp,124,132}).
- `operator*` does not compile if you use it (\src{include/Memory/Janitors.hpp,120}). Use `->` or `Raw()`.
- The use count is a plain `int`: don't share one `TObjectPtr` object between threads.
*/

/**
\class TObjectPtr
\ingroup memory
\brief A reference-counted owning pointer. Copies share the object; the last copy deletes it.

\tparam TObject The type of the object it owns.

Declared at \src{include/Memory/Janitors.hpp,10,122}. See \ref memory for how to use it and what to watch out for.

\genexopen{modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp,Object ptr}
\snippet modules/AssetsAndMemory.cpp object_ptr
\endex
*/

/**
\fn template<typename TCastType, typename TBaseType> TObjectPtr<TCastType> CastTo(const TObjectPtr<TBaseType>& Ptr)
\ingroup memory
\brief Returns a pointer of another type to the same object, sharing its use count. Unchecked: the object must really be a `TCastType`.

Defined at \src{include/Memory/Janitors.hpp,124,132}. `Ptr` must not be empty.

\engex{include/Filesystem/Importers/Importer.hpp,67,69,Returning a cached asset as the requested type}
```cpp
if (Asset->ObjectData.IsValid()) {
    return CastTo<TAssetType>(Asset->ObjectData);
}
```
\endex
*/

/**
\fn void Memory::Module()
\ingroup memory
\brief Empty. The engine does not call it.
*/

/**
\fn void Memory::TeardownModule()
\ingroup memory
\brief Empty. The engine does not call it.
